@@ -1,36 +1,51 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b2.24.01.llevar-gerundio",
-  "title": "Perífrasis durativas: Llevar + gerundio",
+  "title": "Llevar + Gerund",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "La perífrasis aspectual *llevar + gerundio* cuantifica la duración temporal ininterrumpida de una acción o estado que comenzó en el pasado y continúa vigente en el momento de referencia. Exige obligatoriamente la expresión de un complemento temporal (*dos semanas*, *décadas*, *mucho tiempo*). A diferencia de *hace... que*, *llevar + gerundio* pone el foco sintáctico en la persistencia activa del sujeto durante todo ese intervalo acumulado."
+      "content": "You know *desde hace* from A2 Unit 7: *Estudio español desde hace tres años*. Spanish has a second way to say it, with *llevar*: *Llevo tres años estudiando español*. You know *estar* + gerund from A1 Unit 19 (*estoy estudiando*). In this pattern *llevar* takes the place of *estar*, and the length of time comes straight after it, with no word for \"for\" in between. The action began in the past and is still going on now. Because the time comes first, this is the natural choice when you want to stress how long something has taken, or how much effort it has cost.",
+      "title": "How long has it been going on?"
     },
     {
       "type": "table",
-      "title": "Estructura y contrastes de Llevar + gerundio",
+      "title": "llevar + length of time + gerund",
       "rows": [
         [
-          "Llevo tres años investigando la arquitectura de los conventillos porteños.",
-          "I have been researching the architecture of Buenos Aires tenements for three years."
+          "Llevo tres años estudiando español.",
+          "I have been studying Spanish for three years."
         ],
         [
-          "Los arqueólogos urbanos llevaban meses excavando las márgenes del Riachuelo.",
-          "The urban archaeologists had been excavating the banks of the Riachuelo for months."
+          "Llevamos una hora esperando el autobús.",
+          "We have been waiting for the bus for an hour."
         ],
         [
-          "¿Cuánto tiempo llevás viviendo en el barrio de San Telmo?",
-          "How long have you been living in the San Telmo neighborhood?"
+          "¿Cuánto tiempo llevas trabajando aquí?",
+          "How long have you been working here?"
         ],
         [
-          "Llevamos semanas debatiendo la vigencia del psicoanálisis en la cultura popular.",
-          "We have been debating the relevance of psychoanalysis in popular culture for weeks."
+          "Mi hermana lleva mucho tiempo buscando casa.",
+          "My sister has been looking for a house for a long time."
         ]
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In the past, and with sin",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "Llevábamos una hora esperando cuando llegó el tren.",
+          "english": "We had been waiting for an hour when the train arrived."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Llevo una semana sin dormir bien.",
+          "english": "I have not slept well for a week."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Para la negación de acciones prolongadas, se emplea frecuentemente la estructura alternativa *llevar + sin + infinitivo*: *Lleva tres meses sin ensayar con la orquesta típica* ('He has gone three months without rehearsing with the tango orchestra')."
+      "content": "The verb after the time is always a gerund: *estudiando*, never *estudiar* or *estudiado*. To say how long you have gone without doing something, use *sin* + infinitive: *Llevo una semana sin dormir bien*, not *Llevo una semana no durmiendo*. Use *llevar* only for something that is still going on; for a finished action, use the preterite: *Estudié tres años*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b2/b2-24-02-a-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b2/b2-24-02-a-gr.json
