 {
   "id": "grammar.b2.26.02.haber-de-infinitivo",
-  "title": "Modalidad culta: Haber de + infinitivo",
+  "title": "Haber de + Infinitive",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "La perífrasis modal *haber de + infinitivo* pertenece al registro formal y literario. Puede expresar dos valores fundamentales: una obligación o necesidad atenuada equivalente a un deber moral o institucional fijado de antemano (*hemos de reconocer la verdad*), o bien un destino ineludible o prospección fatal que orienta el porvenir de los acontecimientos (*aquel suceso habría de marcar para siempre su destino*)."
+      "content": "You know *haber* from the present perfect (A2 Unit 1): *he*, *has*, *ha*, *hemos*, *han*. The same forms, followed by *de* and an infinitive, make a formal pattern that you mostly read in books, speeches and careful writing. It has two uses. The first is a duty, a little more serious than *tener que*: *He de reconocer que tienes razón* is \"I must admit that you are right\". The second is for stories: *había de* or *habría de* + infinitive says what was going to happen later, as if the writer already knew: *Aquel viaje había de cambiar su vida* is \"That journey was to change his life\".",
+      "title": "A formal pattern"
     },
     {
       "type": "table",
-      "title": "Valores de Haber de + infinitivo en prosa formal",
+      "title": "haber de + infinitive",
       "rows": [
         [
-          "Hemos de admitir que la memoria histórica constituye el cimiento de la democracia.",
-          "We must admit that historical memory constitutes the foundation of democracy."
+          "He de reconocer que tienes razón.",
+          "I must admit that you are right."
         ],
         [
-          "Aquel encuentro en el Salón de Primavera había de desencadenar una tragedia irreparable.",
-          "That meeting at the Spring Salon was destined to unleash an irreparable tragedy."
+          "Los estudiantes han de llegar antes de las nueve.",
+          "Students are to arrive before nine."
         ],
         [
-          "Si has de juzgar mis actos, hazlo examinando las circunstancias que los motivaron.",
-          "If you are to judge my actions, do so by examining the circumstances that motivated them."
+          "Aquel viaje había de cambiar su vida.",
+          "That journey was to change his life."
         ],
         [
-          "Los historiadores habrán de revisar minuciosamente los documentos recién desclasificados.",
-          "Historians will have to scrutinize meticulously the newly declassified documents."
+          "Años después, aquella casa habría de convertirse en un museo.",
+          "Years later, that house was to become a museum."
         ]
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "En pretérito imperfecto (*había de...*) o condicional simple (*habría de...*), esta perífrasis actúa frecuentemente en biografías y relatos históricos para anunciar hechos futuros respecto del tiempo narrativo principal."
+      "content": "Do not confuse *haber de* with *hay que*. *Haber de* changes with the person (*he de*, *has de*, *hemos de*), but *hay que* never does. In everyday speech people say *tengo que* or *debo* instead, so use *haber de* when you want to sound formal or when you tell a story."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b2/b2-26-03-a-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b2/b2-26-03-a-gr.json
