 {
   "id": "grammar.b2.26.03.tener-que-infinitivo",
-  "title": "Necesidad objetiva: Tener que + infinitivo",
+  "title": "Tener que",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "A diferencia de *deber + infinitivo* (que apela a un imperativo ético o moral interno), *tener que + infinitivo* expresa una obligación ineludible impuesta por circunstancias externas, necesidades materiales imperiosas o exigencias institucionales forzosas. En tiempos del pasado (*tuve que declarar*), constata la realización efectiva de la acción forzada."
+      "content": "You know *tener que* + infinitive from A1 Unit 18, and *deber* from B1 Unit 4, where *deber* gives advice and *tener que* is stronger. At B2 the useful question is where the pressure comes from. *Deber* talks about a duty or a moral rule: *Debes ayudar a tus vecinos*. *Tener que* talks about a need that comes from the situation, and the speaker cannot change it: *Tengo que salir ya, porque el tren sale a las siete*. That is why *tener que* so often comes with a reason (*porque*, *si quieres*). It can also be a warm recommendation: *Tienes que probar este pastel* means \"You must try this cake\".",
+      "title": "A need, not a duty"
     },
     {
       "type": "table",
-      "title": "Usos de Tener que en distintos marcos temporales",
+      "title": "tener que",
       "rows": [
         [
-          "Ante la gravedad de la crisis bancaria, el gobierno tuvo que decretar el estado de emergencia.",
-          "Faced with the severity of the banking crisis, the government had to decree a state of emergency."
+          "Tengo que salir ya, porque el tren sale a las siete.",
+          "I have to leave now, because the train leaves at seven."
         ],
         [
-          "Los ciudadanos tuvieron que organizarse en asambleas barriales para sostener comedores comunitarios.",
-          "Citizens had to organize into neighborhood assemblies to sustain community soup kitchens."
+          "Si quieres vivir aquí, tienes que pagar el alquiler.",
+          "If you want to live here, you have to pay the rent."
         ],
         [
-          "Para comprender la magnitud de la tragedia, tendríamos que examinar los legajos de la CONADEP.",
-          "To understand the magnitude of the tragedy, we would have to examine the CONADEP files."
+          "Debes ayudar a tus vecinos.",
+          "You should help your neighbours."
         ],
         [
-          "El testigo tuvo que comparecer ante el tribunal penal bajo estrictas medidas de seguridad.",
-          "The witness had to appear before the criminal court under strict security measures."
+          "Tienes que probar este pastel; está delicioso.",
+          "You have to try this cake; it is delicious."
         ]
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "En enunciados condicionales contraargumentativos, *tener que + infinitivo* en condicional (*tendrías que haberlo sabido*) expresa reproche ante una negligencia o descuido imputable al interlocutor."
+      "content": "*Tener que* always has a person: *tengo que*, *tienes que*, *tiene que*. When no one in particular has to do it, Spanish uses *hay que* (the next lesson). Do not use *tener que* for a rule that is the same for everybody. Later in this unit you will also see *tener que* used for a guess, as in *Tiene que ser él*."
     }
   ]
 }
